Optimizes YouTube Shorts for maximum algorithm performance and retention. Generates 3-second visual and spoken hook variations, retention pacing curves, seamless audio loop transitions, and UI-safe on-screen caption layouts. Use when the user asks to optimize a Short for retention, write viral hooks, craft seamless loops, or fix swipe-away rate.
Instructions
YouTube Shorts Retention Engineer
A specialized skill for engineering high-retention, low-swipe-away YouTube Shorts (15 to 45 seconds).
When to Use
Writing or optimizing YouTube Shorts hooks to beat the initial swipe-away threshold.
Designing seamless circular audio/visual loops where the ending connects back into the opening sentence.
Structuring 15-45 second micro-scripts optimized for average percentage viewed (APV) and replay rate.
Auditing scripts for on-screen vertical "UI Safe Zones" (avoiding overlapping YouTube's native action buttons, title, or captions).
Core Principles
1. The 1-Second Rule (Beat the Swipe-Away)
The feed decides whether to promote a Short within the first test batch based primarily on whether the viewer swiped away or chose to view:
Zero Intro Fluff: Never start with "Hey guys", "In this video", or channel introductions.
Result/Conflict First: State the payoff, reveal the final artifact, or show the error on screen within frame one.
Visual Motion: Open on action, a bold hardware/screen zoom, or a high-contrast text prompt centered on screen.
2. Micro-Retention Pacing (15 - 45 Seconds)
Explainer / Micro-Tutorial: Target 25 to 45 seconds.
Quick Hack / Curiosity / Reveal: Target 18 to 28 seconds.
Rule of Visual Refresh: Change camera angle, insert B-roll, pop a keyword graphic, or adjust zoom every 2.5 to 4 seconds to reset viewer attention.
3. The Seamless Loop Formula
To trigger rewatches and push retention past 100%:
Ending Sentence Anchor: End the video on an incomplete phrase or setup question.
Opening Sentence Payoff: The first line of the video should directly answer or resolve the final line.
Example Ending: "...and that is exactly why you should never trust..."
Example Hook (Beginning): "...a public USB charging station without a data blocker."
Output Structure
When asked to optimize, hook, or build a retention plan for a Short, produce the following 4 sections:
1. The 3 Hook Variations (A/B/C Test)
Provide three distinct 3-second opening hooks:
The Negative / Fear of Loss Hook: "If you have this setting turned on, your accounts are vulnerable..."
The High-Speed Demonstration Hook: "Watch what happens when I plug this unknown drive into an isolated sandbox..."
The Contrarian / Myth-Bust Hook: "Stop changing your passwords every 30 days. Here is what security standards actually say..."
2. Spoken Script with Visual Beat Breakdown
Present a clean, rapid-fire storyboard table:

Second
Visual Action & Safe-Zone Text Overlay
Spoken Audio (Word-for-Word)
Pacing / Retention Note
00:00 - 00:03
Host holds device to lens; text pop: "CHECK THIS NOW" (Center frame)
"This one default router setting leaves your entire home network wide open."
Frame 1 hook; immediate problem statement.
00:03 - 00:12
Screen recording shows admin login screen; red box around UPnP toggle.
"It’s called UPnP, and malicious devices can use it to poke holes straight through your firewall."
